00:04:00.630 --> 00:04:08.820
All right, so just need to get started, I
wanted to go back to the most simplest concept

00:04:08.820 --> 00:04:12.350
in inference, and those that already know
this, bear with me. It will only like five

00:04:12.350 --> 00:04:17.900
minutes. And basically, what I'm going to
do is explain what the little stars on top

00:04:17.900 --> 00:04:22.500
of the buildings with the antenna that we
see in scientific papers means. So that's

00:04:22.500 --> 00:04:30.100
what they put on the graph to relay that it's
statistically significant.

00:04:30.100 --> 00:04:35.730
So I'm going to just go over that for five
minutes and then explain some subtleties how

00:04:35.730 --> 00:04:43.471
this has come up in my research. So to give
a very simple example, suppose we have two

00:04:43.471 --> 00:04:50.650
groups, and basically just to make it simple,
I'm going to use men and women and heights.

00:04:50.650 --> 00:04:57.260
And what I have is a population of men and
women. You can take the mean of the man and

00:04:57.260 --> 00:05:03.600
the mean of women, and those two means may
or may not be different, but suppose we don't

00:05:03.600 --> 00:05:08.780
know. We come from outer space and we don't
know that that's the case. So we want to know,

00:05:08.780 --> 00:05:16.800
and we could just measure everybody and come
up with an answer, or we could take a sample.

00:05:16.800 --> 00:05:22.810
So if we take a sample of 10 people of 10
of each, we then get data. So here's what

00:05:22.810 --> 00:05:26.830
it would look like. Here's an example of a
sample. This is an actual sample that I took

00:05:26.830 --> 00:05:34.641
with using pre-measured heights, and we can
see that there's a difference between men

00:05:34.641 --> 00:05:41.240
and women. Men are taller by three inches
in this sample. So inference is the part of

00:05:41.240 --> 00:05:48.280
statistics that helps you understand if this
is-- how much can this very, and how consistent

00:05:48.280 --> 00:05:51.990
this is with the actual means being the same
or different.

00:05:51.990 --> 00:05:56.820
So we basically are interested in the means
of the population. So we look at the sample

00:05:56.820 --> 00:06:04.160
means and then use that to try to answer the
question. So here's one way to see inference,

00:06:04.160 --> 00:06:08.670
to understand inference. So we did it once.
I'm just going to do it for the mean heights,

00:06:08.670 --> 00:06:16.400
not for the difference for now.
The important concepts to get here is that

00:06:16.400 --> 00:06:21.400
every time we take a sample the mean changes.
Here's the mean of men for the first sample

00:06:21.400 --> 00:06:24.370
I took, but if I take another one, it will
be a different one. I'm going to start making

00:06:24.370 --> 00:06:29.690
a histogram where these height's pile up,
and you see that we get different values every

00:06:29.690 --> 00:06:39.630
time, and we do it a million times. We eventually
you get a distribution of the heights, of

00:06:39.630 --> 00:06:43.270
the average heights.
And we can see how much it varies. It varies

00:06:43.270 --> 00:06:50.680
from around 67 to about 71, as for 95% of
the data is, of the outcomes are. This tells

00:06:50.680 --> 00:06:54.890
us, wait a minute, we saw a difference, but
it could have been different. How different

00:06:54.890 --> 00:06:59.150
could it have been? Well, this distribution
helps us understand that, and that's the general

00:06:59.150 --> 00:07:06.870
principle behind inference. But understanding
that the population has variability, and that

00:07:06.870 --> 00:07:10.770
there's tall men and short men, et cetera,
and the fact that we take a sample results

00:07:10.770 --> 00:07:15.210
in the average heights having variability
as well.

00:07:15.210 --> 00:07:19.169
So if we make the sample bigger, this is one
of the things we learn statistic classes,

00:07:19.169 --> 00:07:24.250
then the variance of that mean gets smaller.
So you can see that this distribution is not

00:07:24.250 --> 00:07:29.810
as fat. So this is what you statistic classes,
that that mean and those means, both for men

00:07:29.810 --> 00:07:34.200
and woman, is going to be approximately normal
with the population mean, standard deviation

00:07:34.200 --> 00:07:39.310
divided by the square root of the sample size.
The way we use this in biology very often

00:07:39.310 --> 00:07:46.740
is by constructing a t statistic, and we can
do it using some mathematics. Once we model

00:07:46.740 --> 00:07:50.770
them, we know how much that t statistic should
vary just by chance, and from there we get

00:07:50.770 --> 00:07:55.330
P values, and confidence intervals, et cetera.
So that's just a reminder of what we're talking

00:07:55.330 --> 00:08:00.490
about here. Sometimes we don't assume normality,
we use a model and assume the t distribution.

00:08:00.490 --> 00:08:06.190
I'm not getting into that yet.
OK. So, I'm going to give you an example of

00:08:06.190 --> 00:08:14.130
how that simple concept has-- this was a research
activity of mine a long time ago, and this

00:08:14.130 --> 00:08:21.860
actually keeps coming up, but how the concept
was not being clearly understood by collaborators

00:08:21.860 --> 00:08:27.310
and other people publishing papers, and we
did a little experiment to kind of point out

00:08:27.310 --> 00:08:30.990
the problem. So I'm going to give you an example,
and it relates to the decision sometimes made

00:08:30.990 --> 00:08:36.919
by biologists or experimental biologists to
pool their samples to reduce variance.

00:08:36.919 --> 00:08:45.300
OK, so here's an experimental design that
is very common in biology. Let's say now,

00:08:45.300 --> 00:08:50.300
let's talk about gene expression. That's one
of the topics that I work in. So we have a

00:08:50.300 --> 00:08:56.480
microarray and we're going to get gene expression
for 12,000 genes. For each gene we get 12

00:08:56.480 --> 00:09:01.730
measurements. Now I'm going to just focus
the discussion on if we had just one gene

00:09:01.730 --> 00:09:10.540
or two genes. We have several mice. What we
want to find out is if a gene is differentially

00:09:10.540 --> 00:09:15.480
expressed in two different strains.
So we follow the procedure we just talked

00:09:15.480 --> 00:09:19.550
about with the heights. We get 12 mice of
each strain, we're going to measure the gene

00:09:19.550 --> 00:09:24.500
expression for a given gene, and then try
to answer that question. So if we say yes,

00:09:24.500 --> 00:09:29.100
then we say yes, this is differentially expressed,
then what we're saying is if you do this experiment

00:09:29.100 --> 00:09:34.260
in your lab, you will also
see it be different. That's kind of what we're

00:09:34.260 --> 00:09:39.490
saying.
Many of my collaborators were doing something

00:09:39.490 --> 00:09:44.061
that actually kept them from answering that
question. How was that the case? Because they

00:09:44.061 --> 00:09:48.930
pooled the data. They pooled the data from
the 12 mice, and then they created four technical

00:09:48.930 --> 00:09:54.430
replicates. When I asked why, they will say
because that reduces the variance. OK, if

00:09:54.430 --> 00:09:58.240
you're doing that, that means you don't understand
statistical inference, and I'm going to explain

00:09:58.240 --> 00:10:03.430
why that is. This is the experimental design.
We take 12 measurements with 12 mice, pool

00:10:03.430 --> 00:10:06.150
them, and then create four replicates of the
pool.

00:10:06.150 --> 00:10:13.040
Once you do that, you will not see the variability
across the mice. Imagine if you're doing this

00:10:13.040 --> 00:10:22.610
with heights, that would be taking 10 men,
measuring their height, measuring their heights

00:10:22.610 --> 00:10:27.430
10 times, and then reporting the average of
the 10 four times. So if you just presented

00:10:27.430 --> 00:10:31.240
this to someone who didn't know anything about
heights, they would think that there's no

00:10:31.240 --> 00:10:36.850
tall men and short men. There's just one man,
and there's a little bit of measurement error

00:10:36.850 --> 00:10:41.510
when we measure it.
So here's an example. This is not a cartoon.

00:10:41.510 --> 00:10:46.501
This is data from the experiment of two of
the genes in the microarray. These are two

00:10:46.501 --> 00:10:54.490
genes that when they look at, they compare
the two groups, the two pairs of four replicates

00:10:54.490 --> 00:10:59.160
that were coming from technical replicas.
These four and four, that's how you see four

00:10:59.160 --> 00:11:04.899
points, four and four. We get these two genes
with very, very small t values, the chance

00:11:04.899 --> 00:11:09.220
of seeing something like this by chance using
this t statistic we just described is very,

00:11:09.220 --> 00:11:13.279
very small. It's definitely significant. Both
genes are like that.

00:11:13.279 --> 00:11:21.360
So we write we write a paper and we say, these
two [INAUDIBLE] expressed. If someone else

00:11:21.360 --> 00:11:26.230
runs this experiment, are they going to see
this again when they do it? And the answer

00:11:26.230 --> 00:11:30.510
is we actually don't know. Why not? Because
now I'm going to show you the original data.

00:11:30.510 --> 00:11:36.399
Now I'm going to show you this. This is actually
an experiment we ran to kind of make this

00:11:36.399 --> 00:11:40.760
point. I'm actually going to share this data,
because in the experiment we ran, we actually

00:11:40.760 --> 00:11:45.339
kept both. We kept these 12 measurements,
these 12 measurements, these four, and these

00:11:45.339 --> 00:11:49.290
four.
So now look at those two things. So that is

00:11:49.290 --> 00:11:55.050
now telling a very different story. The first
gene has a lot of variability, and the second

00:11:55.050 --> 00:12:02.240
one has less variability. This is a much--
you say it's more consistent. So now when

00:12:02.240 --> 00:12:08.940
I do it like this, only the second gene has
a significant p-value. The first one doesn't.

00:12:08.940 --> 00:12:13.490
So if someone ran this experiment again, they
would actually not confirm gene one, but they

00:12:13.490 --> 00:12:17.700
probably will if they have enough power. They
probably will confirm gene two.

00:12:17.700 --> 00:12:23.680
So the lesson here is you want to see the
variation because for the inference you're

00:12:23.680 --> 00:12:35.770
doing, it is important. It's part of the framework
of inference that you do for this. This is

00:12:35.770 --> 00:12:41.270
something that comes up over and over again,
where the variability that's important to

00:12:41.270 --> 00:12:48.440
inference is not understood and many mistakes
are made because of that error. So here are

00:12:48.440 --> 00:12:54.620
the two genes next to each other. There's
another example, I think, of two things that

00:12:54.620 --> 00:12:57.720
are like that
where one is very consistent, the other one

00:12:57.720 --> 00:13:00.550
isn't, but when you pool you don't really
get to see that.

00:13:00.550 --> 00:13:05.700
We actually wrote a paper on this. It's not
just about this. It has other things I experiment.

00:13:05.700 --> 00:13:11.340
It's about experimental designs, when you
need to pool-- in that case, it actually explains

00:13:11.340 --> 00:13:19.680
this in more detail, and it's published in
2005 in PNAS. OK. So one of the morals of

00:13:19.680 --> 00:13:27.380
the story of that particular example is that
the biological variability, it's much bigger

00:13:27.380 --> 00:13:33.060
than technical variability. I think that's
going to be common in many, many of the measurement

00:13:33.060 --> 00:13:38.089
technologies that we have in big data and
biology in general. That we're going to get

00:13:38.089 --> 00:13:43.380
more and more precise technical measurements,
but human variability, or whatever we're measuring,

00:13:43.380 --> 00:13:49.240
won't go away.
Another thing I would like to mention in our

00:13:49.240 --> 00:13:55.561
class about inference, in a talk about inference,
is the multiple comparison problem. This is

00:13:55.561 --> 00:14:03.120
obvious to most, but I still see papers coming
out that do not correct for multiple comparisons

00:14:03.120 --> 00:14:11.660
in any way, and so I want to just briefly
mention it. I guess I just awkwardly use these

00:14:11.660 --> 00:14:19.900
slides. I'll just described describe the problem.
In the case of gene expression, you have high

00:14:19.900 --> 00:14:27.140
throughput-- sorry, in the case of high throughput
technology, we are-- and we're trying to find,

00:14:27.140 --> 00:14:30.770
say, differential expression, differentially
expressed genes. It could be other things.

00:14:30.770 --> 00:14:35.510
It could be differentially methylated CPGs,
it could be differentially binding transcription

00:14:35.510 --> 00:14:43.260
factor binding sites, et cetera.
We're exploring a big space. We're testing

00:14:43.260 --> 00:14:47.370
my hypothesis. That's how a statistician would
say it, and one thing to keep in mind is that

00:14:47.370 --> 00:14:55.490
if you test 10,000 hypotheses and you restrict
the genes you report, or whatever units you

00:14:55.490 --> 00:15:10.310
report to 5% p-value, you're going to get
500 incorrect reportings when the null hypothesis

00:15:10.310 --> 00:15:16.642
is true.
So if you don't understand-- many of you probably

00:15:16.642 --> 00:15:20.370
are thinking, yeah, I know that, but if you
don't know exactly what I'm talking about

00:15:20.370 --> 00:15:25.600
here, definitely go read up on multiple comparison.
We have a course that covers it. It's in the

00:15:25.600 --> 00:15:29.420
book, but there's many other books and other
courses it that describe ti.

00:15:29.420 --> 00:15:36.050
All right, so that's the first part of the
talk. I know it was very basic. Now, I want

00:15:36.050 --> 00:15:43.459
to go to some more complicated topics, the
complex topics, where statistical modeling

00:15:43.459 --> 00:15:50.029
has helped and also exploratory data analysis,
which is I know it's not in the title of the

00:15:50.029 --> 00:15:56.490
topic, but it's as important or perhaps more
important, I think, in the analysis of what

00:15:56.490 --> 00:16:03.610
today we're calling big data. I'll do through
examples in the second part.

00:16:03.610 --> 00:16:17.810
OK, so the first thing I want to warn against
is our desire for wanting to have workflows.

00:16:17.810 --> 00:16:25.070
This is a screenshot of what you get on Google
Images when you Google workflows and biology,

00:16:25.070 --> 00:16:33.140
or in biomedical sciences. You get a bunch
of things like this, but if you look at them

00:16:33.140 --> 00:16:40.881
closely, many of them basically come down
to a workflow like this, describing a way

00:16:40.881 --> 00:16:48.190
to go from big data to knowledge. That's the
title of the graph. So you start with big

00:16:48.190 --> 00:16:53.130
data and then you go through this process,
it's the use of boxes, and at the end there's

00:16:53.130 --> 00:16:59.900
some list, or some figure, and
you're done. You discover something or learn

00:16:59.900 --> 00:17:07.119
something.
One of my goals here is to remind listeners

00:17:07.119 --> 00:17:15.509
of how important it is to think hard about
what these boxes are doing, and sometimes

00:17:15.509 --> 00:17:20.730
make sure that they're doing what we think
we're doing. It's many times the case that

00:17:20.730 --> 00:17:25.949
the procedures that we think are standard
procedure, and we can just stick them into

00:17:25.949 --> 00:17:31.009
the workflow will work, and it turns out that
just by looking at a few plots, we quickly

00:17:31.009 --> 00:17:36.691
realize that it's absolutely not the case.
That this actually leads to knowledge, instead

00:17:36.691 --> 00:17:43.289
it leads to false positives and confusion.
So what I want to do is I want to explain

00:17:43.289 --> 00:17:49.259
examples of where this workflow that people
were using without exploring data, just by

00:17:49.259 --> 00:17:56.399
kind of turning the crank, we're leading to
problems. All right, so here's a quote from

00:17:56.399 --> 00:18:05.019
John Tukey, that kind of relates to what I
just described. All right, so to motivate

00:18:05.019 --> 00:18:12.580
this, I want to-- I also want to motivate
the improvement of the boxes in the workflow.

00:18:12.580 --> 00:18:16.889
Not just saying we're going to keep you from
making mistakes, but we actually want to help

00:18:16.889 --> 00:18:23.609
you make discoveries. It's not just about
being policemen or about being a Debbie downer

00:18:23.609 --> 00:18:29.029
about what people find, but rather two things.
You'll have less false positives, but also

00:18:29.029 --> 00:18:35.210
you will have more true positives, hopefully.
To motivate that out, I always like to go

00:18:35.210 --> 00:18:40.840
back to the discovery of the microscope, which--
not the discovery of the microscope, the improvement

00:18:40.840 --> 00:18:46.710
of the microscope, which led the person who
first improved the microscope enough, it led

00:18:46.710 --> 00:18:53.440
him to become the father of microbiology.
So simply by-- no, not simply, but when people

00:18:53.440 --> 00:18:58.080
were looking through microscopes, other scientists
were trying to discover things, but they were

00:18:58.080 --> 00:19:03.539
seeing blurry images. He improved the microscope
and he was able to see something that nobody

00:19:03.539 --> 00:19:09.190
else saw, and now he discovers microbes. He
discovered are many, many other things that

00:19:09.190 --> 00:19:12.429
are microscopic because he was the only one
that could see them.

00:19:12.429 --> 00:19:18.129
So in my field, in high throughput biology,
I feel like the data scientists are often

00:19:18.129 --> 00:19:23.779
not just keeping people from making false
claims, but also helping people put their

00:19:23.779 --> 00:19:30.330
data into focus and make discoveries. Here's
another quote from Tukey related to that particular

00:19:30.330 --> 00:19:37.019
topic. When it comes to data, which is what
we look at now, we don't look through microscopes

00:19:37.019 --> 00:19:42.841
anymore, although some people do, we look
at data. We have digital data that the data

00:19:42.841 --> 00:19:49.789
scientists turn into images, that then biologists
or other scientists turn into knowledge or

00:19:49.789 --> 00:19:57.080
extract knowledge from. And making figures
is exploring data, is a key component of doing

00:19:57.080 --> 00:20:01.299
that properly.
So I'll give you now, concrete examples from

00:20:01.299 --> 00:20:09.769
my own research related to what I just described.
I'm going to talk about two problems, two

00:20:09.769 --> 00:20:13.989
examples, and there's many more, which I won't
have time to talk about. If you want to read

00:20:13.989 --> 00:20:19.369
more about my own personal experience in this,
a lot of my current papers relate to this

00:20:19.369 --> 00:20:26.470
particular topic, and you're of course, welcome
to look some of those up. And I think it's

00:20:26.470 --> 00:20:31.789
happening all over the sciences. It's not
just in genomics. It's not just in biology,

00:20:31.789 --> 00:20:37.359
but improvement of data analysis [INAUDIBLE]
by careful data exploration and

00:20:37.359 --> 00:20:45.380
thinking of how to properly model data, is
leading to a lot of improvements in how we

00:20:45.380 --> 00:20:48.059
interpret data.
OK, so here's an example. This is again, not

00:20:48.059 --> 00:20:53.762
a cartoon, this is actual data. This is actual
experimental data. I'll explain what it is

00:20:53.762 --> 00:21:00.270
now. So the data that I'm presenting in this
plot comes from public repositories. This

00:21:00.270 --> 00:21:09.610
particular one is a gene expression omnibus,
and this is a great resource. It has any published

00:21:09.610 --> 00:21:15.309
gene expression experiment is supposed to
be in either Geo, or in a European database

00:21:15.309 --> 00:21:18.450
called Array Express.
So you can go there, and what I did for this

00:21:18.450 --> 00:21:23.789
one is I went there I got data from five different
tissues. So there's five different tissues

00:21:23.789 --> 00:21:29.690
represented in this figure, and I obtained
gene expression data from them from microarrays,

00:21:29.690 --> 00:21:39.009
in this case. So that means for each sample
I have 12,000 measurements. So it's a 12,000

00:21:39.009 --> 00:21:45.059
number vector, and I have more than five points
because for each tissue I had several individuals.

00:21:45.059 --> 00:21:51.690
So there's about 50 points because for each
issue, I have about 10 people on average.

00:21:51.690 --> 00:22:01.059
So I have these factors. I have about 50 factors
that are in 10,000 plus dimensions right because

00:22:01.059 --> 00:22:05.729
I have one number for each gene. So that's
something I can't really plot, so instead

00:22:05.729 --> 00:22:09.120
I'm going to plot the first two principal
components-- that's what I'm showing here--

00:22:09.120 --> 00:22:13.940
of that big matrix. If you don't know what
principle components are, it's a very, very

00:22:13.940 --> 00:22:21.479
simple explanation of one of the things you
can do with them.

00:22:21.479 --> 00:22:28.210
Each point here represents one of the samples
that is originally 12,000 dimensions. I have

00:22:28.210 --> 00:22:35.840
reduced it to two dimensions. It was 12,000.
And the nice property about this particular

00:22:35.840 --> 00:22:42.120
summery is that the distance between any two
points resembles the distance between the

00:22:42.120 --> 00:22:49.109
original 12,000 dimension vector. So now when
I have it in two dimensions, I can actually

00:22:49.109 --> 00:22:53.860
see what's close and what's not close.
So in this plot I can see that these points

00:22:53.860 --> 00:22:59.200
are close, and then we have these points are
close, these points are close, et cetera.

00:22:59.200 --> 00:23:03.169
So these points are close, so I would tend
to think, well, those are probably from the

00:23:03.169 --> 00:23:07.049
same tissues. Different tissues have different
gene expression patterns. That's one of the

00:23:07.049 --> 00:23:12.970
reasons they become different tissues in the
first place. So I should see here, five clusters.

00:23:12.970 --> 00:23:18.480
I don't quite see them. Now, I'm going to
reveal what the tissues are, and you can see

00:23:18.480 --> 00:23:22.559
that it's not really what we were expecting.
So these guys that were close together are

00:23:22.559 --> 00:23:27.400
different tissues. These are colon and these
are placenta. And we have some colons down

00:23:27.400 --> 00:23:32.960
here that are way farther from these colons
then they are from here. So you can see how

00:23:32.960 --> 00:23:39.059
there are problems in the data. So I would
say that this data is out of focus, or this

00:23:39.059 --> 00:23:43.470
fact that we thought we knew about gene expression
in tissues is maybe not true. So if you just

00:23:43.470 --> 00:23:49.739
go down the pipeline workflow, and run this
data through it, and ask for clusters, you

00:23:49.739 --> 00:23:56.539
conclude biologists are wrong. Gene expression
between tissues is not that different, and

00:23:56.539 --> 00:24:01.600
that would be a mistake.
So because if we then dig deeper into the

00:24:01.600 --> 00:24:05.649
data, forget the workflow, let's go back to
the data and dig deeper into

00:24:05.649 --> 00:24:09.279
it, we're going to find that there is a lot
of noise in the measurement. So now we're

00:24:09.279 --> 00:24:14.470
going to take one point, and instead of just
reducing to the two dimensions, we're actually

00:24:14.470 --> 00:24:21.599
going to look at all the data, all the 12,000
points. And this figure shows you that.

00:24:21.599 --> 00:24:26.610
So now we have the 12,000 points. You can
see there's 12,000 little black dots, and

00:24:26.610 --> 00:24:31.889
the plot is made in a way that reveals what
we're interested in when we look at compared

00:24:31.889 --> 00:24:37.899
two samples to see what genes are different
and which ones are not. We have the log ratio

00:24:37.899 --> 00:24:44.509
of the two measurements on the y-axis. So
for example, this gene is 16. It's log based

00:24:44.509 --> 00:24:52.159
two. This gene is 16 times bigger on one sample
compared to the other, and these that are

00:24:52.159 --> 00:24:59.799
at zero are the same. The log ratio is zero.
So this particular dataset is not one of the

00:24:59.799 --> 00:25:06.229
ones that you just saw. This is from an experiment
that a company ran, and they were nice enough

00:25:06.229 --> 00:25:11.370
to share it with us just to test out the technology.
And what was done for this experiment was

00:25:11.370 --> 00:25:19.559
they took two samples that were the same.
How did they know they were the same? Because

00:25:19.559 --> 00:25:29.090
they took one RNA sample, a vat of material
that had RNA molecules in it, and they randomly

00:25:29.090 --> 00:25:33.640
split it into two. They just split into two
equal parts, and then they ran the experiment

00:25:33.640 --> 00:25:38.580
on those two.
So if the technology is working well, there's

00:25:38.580 --> 00:25:44.499
enough molecules there that things will average
out and it should be the case. And when we

00:25:44.499 --> 00:25:49.409
compare them, when we measure gene expression
in these two, it's basically the same sample.

00:25:49.409 --> 00:25:55.460
We should get the same answer for every gene.
So this mass of points should really be around

00:25:55.460 --> 00:25:58.850
zero, or close to zero.
Now, you can say well, sure, there's measurement

00:25:58.850 --> 00:26:05.460
error. OK, that's very possible. However,
the people who ran this experiment included

00:26:05.460 --> 00:26:11.470
16 genes. These are the orange ones. They're
represented in orange here, where they knew

00:26:11.470 --> 00:26:16.450
there were actually different. They made him
twice as big for real. They actually artificially

00:26:16.450 --> 00:26:23.239
made them twice as big in one sample compared
to the other. So these 12 points are the ones

00:26:23.239 --> 00:26:27.799
that should be different.
The black point should be at zero and these

00:26:27.799 --> 00:26:31.740
black points should be at one. So this gives
you an idea of what signal looks like. This

00:26:31.740 --> 00:26:37.149
is a problem now. If the signal is this big,
then this is a problem. Because if I gave

00:26:37.149 --> 00:26:42.019
you this picture and I said find me the 20
most differential genes, you would probably

00:26:42.019 --> 00:26:46.669
say, well, this one and that one, and this
one and these. These are all big. You would

00:26:46.669 --> 00:26:52.690
never come down to these 60.
So there's a lot of noise in the data. So

00:26:52.690 --> 00:26:58.620
why is that? Why is there so much noise in
the data? Is this just the way biology is?

00:26:58.620 --> 00:27:04.570
Or is this the way that the technology works?
And if it doesn't, we might as well stop buying

00:27:04.570 --> 00:27:12.470
this product. Well, so, to answer this question
one of the things we did was go even one step

00:27:12.470 --> 00:27:20.460
further down the data. These points actually
come from even rawer data, and we read how

00:27:20.460 --> 00:27:24.379
that was done, how they actually got to here,
and we noticed that there was some modeling

00:27:24.379 --> 00:27:30.299
going on. There were statistical modeling
happening when they analyzed the data to get

00:27:30.299 --> 00:27:35.929
to these points.
Here's sort of what the model was for doing

00:27:35.929 --> 00:27:43.679
this. So what was happening is that these
measurements, the manufacturers realized that

00:27:43.679 --> 00:27:48.710
when they were trying to measure the expression
of a specific gene, they were also measuring

00:27:48.710 --> 00:27:54.349
background noise. So even when a gene was
not expressed, the unit that was measuring

00:27:54.349 --> 00:28:02.309
that gene would give values, big values sometimes.
And they also noticed that different measurement

00:28:02.309 --> 00:28:07.130
units produced different background levels,
which was a problem because now when you have

00:28:07.130 --> 00:28:12.200
different gene expression measurements in
two different genes, the only reason you might

00:28:12.200 --> 00:28:19.899
be seeing that is because you just had two
units that worked that way. One measured high

00:28:19.899 --> 00:28:25.119
background, the other mentioned low background.
So they thought of it like this. The model

00:28:25.119 --> 00:28:29.080
they thought about was like this. You wanted
to estimate gene expression, here represented

00:28:29.080 --> 00:28:34.769
with a beta, but there's background that's
represented with the alpha, and every single

00:28:34.769 --> 00:28:40.049
measurement, every single different measurement
unit had a different alpha. So it wasn't like

00:28:40.049 --> 00:28:44.519
a global alpha they can remove. You had to
remove an alpha for each single measurement.

00:28:44.519 --> 00:28:49.809
Every unit had a different one. Every measurement
unit had a different one.

00:28:49.809 --> 00:28:55.710
If you see your measurement as seven, you
don't know if that's four plus three or two

00:28:55.710 --> 00:29:01.769
plus five. You have no way of knowing that.
So to determine that, what they did was they

00:29:01.769 --> 00:29:10.350
said, let's add measurement units to this
instrument that measures just the background.

00:29:10.350 --> 00:29:14.859
So now we have two measurements. One measures
the background plus the signal, the other

00:29:14.859 --> 00:29:18.419
one just measures the background, and what
we'll do then is we'll subtract it and we'll

00:29:18.419 --> 00:29:25.259
get B, and we're done. Problem solved.
But here's the problem with that model. We

00:29:25.259 --> 00:29:32.159
did another experiment just to get an idea
of how off this model was. The problem here

00:29:32.159 --> 00:29:40.519
is that there's no stochasticity described
in the model. We ran an experiment where we

00:29:40.519 --> 00:29:45.019
knew there was no beta. We ran an experiment
that was just a negative control, and in that

00:29:45.019 --> 00:29:51.059
case we should just be seeing alpha and alpha.
So here's a plot of that and you can see that

00:29:51.059 --> 00:29:56.769
it's not right on the line. It's correlated.
That's good. But it's quite a bit of noise.

00:29:56.769 --> 00:30:01.619
This is in the log scale, so that's quite
a bit of noise that you're seeing there between

00:30:01.619 --> 00:30:11.559
Y1 and Y2's measurement of alpha.
So the problem with this is that the actual

00:30:11.559 --> 00:30:15.559
model should look something like this since
there's-- Now this is actually a statistical

00:30:15.559 --> 00:30:22.070
model where there's randomness included. We
have alpha 1, alpha 2, not just alpha and

00:30:22.070 --> 00:30:25.039
alpha. They're different quantities. They're
correlated. In the log scale, the correlations

00:30:25.039 --> 00:30:31.429
is about 0.7. You can see that's the correlation
there is about 0.7. So if that's the case,

00:30:31.429 --> 00:30:36.359
if that's the model, that's a model that to
us makes more sense.

00:30:36.359 --> 00:30:42.719
That implies that if you then look at things
log rations, which is what people were using

00:30:42.719 --> 00:30:49.929
to quantify differences in
gene expression, then the variance of the

00:30:49.929 --> 00:30:54.299
log of that difference-- because that's what
they're using as a measurement, the difference

00:30:54.299 --> 00:30:58.669
between Y1 and Y2. The variance of the log
of that difference we can show mathematically.

00:30:58.669 --> 00:31:02.650
I'm not going to show you here. It's using
something called Taylor's expansion. It's

00:31:02.650 --> 00:31:10.070
not important. We can show that variance is
proportional to the reciprocal of the signal

00:31:10.070 --> 00:31:16.409
squared. That's mathematics. That's not biology.
That's not problems with the technology. It's

00:31:16.409 --> 00:31:21.489
the problem with decision they made, the mathematical
decision they made of how to correct this

00:31:21.489 --> 00:31:26.519
measurement.
So if you look at this formula, then you can

00:31:26.519 --> 00:31:32.210
see that you see it here. If you draw a line
of the variance here, you just go by the points.

00:31:32.210 --> 00:31:38.529
If you draw a line of the variance it would
go like that. That function is 1 over x squared.

00:31:38.529 --> 00:31:45.339
Well, it could be. We would need some constant,
but it's very consistent with what the math

00:31:45.339 --> 00:31:50.979
is predicting. One over b squared, one over
b squared. This would be b, right? This is

00:31:50.979 --> 00:31:57.340
the signal. The average of the two measurements
is an approximation of the signal.

00:31:57.340 --> 00:32:04.759
So that in mind, now that we have a model,
we can come up with a better statistical solution

00:32:04.759 --> 00:32:09.549
that doesn't have this property. We can do
that. We have a model in place, now we can

00:32:09.549 --> 00:32:17.639
do math and figure out, OK, what transformation
of the data would estimate data without having

00:32:17.639 --> 00:32:22.370
a massive variance? And we might introduce
a little bit of bias just to reduce the variance,

00:32:22.370 --> 00:32:26.940
that's one approach that is often used in
statistics. In machine learning that's called

00:32:26.940 --> 00:32:34.429
shrinkage or regularization.
when we do that, with that in mind, we can

00:32:34.429 --> 00:32:40.500
construct a solution based on the model, and
we can go from this plot to this plot. That's

00:32:40.500 --> 00:32:47.620
without any new experiment, nothing new. Just
the same data processed differently. We get

00:32:47.620 --> 00:32:51.960
rid of all that noise that we were seeing
earlier, and now if we asked you to give me

00:32:51.960 --> 00:32:56.769
the biggest differences, you would definitely
start with some of these orange points. So

00:32:56.769 --> 00:33:01.339
that's quite a big improvement.
Again, that was just the result of changing

00:33:01.339 --> 00:33:07.080
the way you model the data and developed statistical
solutions for it. So that idea was turned

00:33:07.080 --> 00:33:13.749
into software, and now today, when people
use these technologies, most-- still not everybody,

00:33:13.749 --> 00:33:19.129
but most I would say, use a technique like
this rather than what was originally proposed.

00:33:19.129 --> 00:33:28.830
All right, so that's one example of how statistical
models can improve measurement data that you

00:33:28.830 --> 00:33:34.969
would then use to make this biological discovery.
For example, let's go back to the motivation

00:33:34.969 --> 00:33:41.779
example. Here are the data that you started
with, which I would say is really out of focus

00:33:41.779 --> 00:33:48.690
data. This is processed with the original
algorithm. Once I use this new idea that comes

00:33:48.690 --> 00:33:56.460
from a more rigorous statistical model, you
end up with a plot that looks like this, and

00:33:56.460 --> 00:34:04.710
that is now much more consistent with what
a biologist told us. Gene expression parents

00:34:04.710 --> 00:34:08.109
from the same tissue are similar to each other,
and they're different from different tissues.

00:34:08.109 --> 00:34:12.659
We see the five clusters very clearly. If
we didn't know that, we would actually discover

00:34:12.659 --> 00:34:17.270
it because if you looked at this data without
the colors, you would be pretty sure there's

00:34:17.270 --> 00:34:20.590
four, maybe five
clusters, and you would be onto something.

00:34:20.590 --> 00:34:30.230
All right, so now moving on to another way
in which statistical modeling helps us. Quite

00:34:30.230 --> 00:34:36.250
different. I like to talk about this one because
it's-- I think, currently, the biggest challenge

00:34:36.250 --> 00:34:41.830
in genomics is dealing with what we call the
batch effect, and I suspect we're going to

00:34:41.830 --> 00:34:47.510
see batch effects in pretty much any kind
of measurement technology that is collected

00:34:47.510 --> 00:34:51.600
across time. You'll see what I mean as I as
I describe this.

00:34:51.600 --> 00:35:00.110
OK, so I'm going to use an example. This is
a paper that used the standard workflow. They

00:35:00.110 --> 00:35:06.980
followed all the best practices. They had
raw data, they processed it with the recommended

00:35:06.980 --> 00:35:13.930
processing algorithm, they did the t-tests
correctly, then they did Bonferroni correction.

00:35:13.930 --> 00:35:19.660
So they dealt with a multiple comparison problem
in a very conservative way, and even after

00:35:19.660 --> 00:35:32.050
that they report. Even after that conservative
use of Bonferroni to not make type I errors,

00:35:32.050 --> 00:35:41.160
they conclude that 1,097 of the 4,197 genes
that they tested show a statistically significant

00:35:41.160 --> 00:35:47.270
difference between two different ethnic groups
when you measure gene expression in blood.

00:35:47.270 --> 00:35:55.280
So that's a big number. It's contrary to what
most people would think was the case. I think

00:35:55.280 --> 00:35:59.820
if you ask somebody before this paper was
published how many genes are differentially

00:35:59.820 --> 00:36:06.880
expressed between two ethnic groups, they
would probably say between 0 and maybe five.

00:36:06.880 --> 00:36:11.940
That's what I would have answered. Instead
they get 1,000. So they got their paper published

00:36:11.940 --> 00:36:19.240
in a very prominent biological journal because
it's a surprising result.

00:36:19.240 --> 00:36:25.100
All right, so now let's forget the workflow
and let's go back to the data and look at

00:36:25.100 --> 00:36:32.680
it. So you can actually get this data from
Geo. This is the data from that paper. You

00:36:32.680 --> 00:36:39.900
can see there is about 200 points, 209 I think.
So each point now, it's not a different issue,

00:36:39.900 --> 00:36:47.030
each point is a different person, and each
person has a profile of-- they say out of

00:36:47.030 --> 00:36:52.120
4,000, but I think the original number was
8,000 that they filter down. So this is a

00:36:52.120 --> 00:36:57.600
technology that measured about 8,000 gene
expression genes. Mapped expression for 8,000

00:36:57.600 --> 00:37:05.550
genes.
For each person you have a vector of 8,000,

00:37:05.550 --> 00:37:10.880
and then we reduce it down to two, PCA just
take a first look at the data, and we can

00:37:10.880 --> 00:37:16.020
see that the results here is very consistent
with what they were saying about 1,000 genes

00:37:16.020 --> 00:37:20.170
being different because there is a very clear
difference between the two ethnic groups being

00:37:20.170 --> 00:37:25.640
compared. You have the orange and it's compared
against the green. The first principal component

00:37:25.640 --> 00:37:33.750
is very different. That to me is very consistent
with what was published.

00:37:33.750 --> 00:37:40.850
Now, looking at this data there's something
else that catches our eye, and it's that we

00:37:40.850 --> 00:37:48.691
see other clusters, not just the ethnic groups.
If you look closely at the orange group, which

00:37:48.691 --> 00:37:53.780
is all the same ethnic group, you see-- I
see at least, I see a cluster here and a cluster

00:37:53.780 --> 00:37:57.750
here, and then I move here, there's another
cluster here, and you can kind of see another

00:37:57.750 --> 00:38:03.831
one here. But regardless of that, if this
group shows this much variation, why is this

00:38:03.831 --> 00:38:09.110
group showing this much variation? That's
a lot more. That's double or triple.

00:38:09.110 --> 00:38:15.010
So when we see a picture that, you start wondering
what else could explain this difference? And

00:38:15.010 --> 00:38:19.960
in this case, there's a group of scientists.
It wasn't my group, it was another group that

00:38:19.960 --> 00:38:25.880
decided to noted-- I don't know how they originally
noted this, but they noted that these samples

00:38:25.880 --> 00:38:31.320
were processed across five or six years, and
it turns out that when you get the raw data

00:38:31.320 --> 00:38:36.470
for this, you can actually get the date. It's
actually stamped on the file where the data

00:38:36.470 --> 00:38:40.570
lives.
Now I'm going to show you the same plot. Points

00:38:40.570 --> 00:38:46.240
aren't going to move. All I'm going to do
is change the color to represent date, year,

00:38:46.240 --> 00:38:53.100
instead of ethnic group. OK, so now when I
do that, I can see that those two clusters

00:38:53.100 --> 00:38:57.320
that I kind of could see before, right here
and here, one is greenish and the other one

00:38:57.320 --> 00:39:04.860
is the orange. That's 2002 and this 2003.
So that was the moment in which these measurements

00:39:04.860 --> 00:39:10.691
were made. Not when the people were born or
anything like that. It's just technical. Then

00:39:10.691 --> 00:39:19.250
we see 2004 here, 2005, 2006.
So, is this variability that we see described

00:39:19.250 --> 00:39:25.170
by-- is it really just the date? The batch?
We call them batch effects because these are

00:39:25.170 --> 00:39:30.890
things that were processed in batches. It
wasn't just done once. So this particular

00:39:30.890 --> 00:39:36.860
experiment, it's a little bit hard to distinguish
between ethnicity and date because it's confounded.

00:39:36.860 --> 00:39:43.020
If you go back and forth, you see that the
orange points coincide with the green and

00:39:43.020 --> 00:39:52.410
orange, here, and that the green coincide
with the pink. So they were basically done

00:39:52.410 --> 00:39:56.880
in different years. So when you compare two
things, it's hard to tell is it year or is

00:39:56.880 --> 00:40:03.420
it ethnicity?
You can do more data exploration, and if you

00:40:03.420 --> 00:40:11.000
do I think you'll be convinced that it's probably
date what's making this so variable. Here's

00:40:11.000 --> 00:40:16.170
one example. This is the first principal component
stratified. Now I'm going further. Instead

00:40:16.170 --> 00:40:22.040
of year, I'm doing it by month. So this is
one ethnic group, here, all these orange ones,

00:40:22.040 --> 00:40:31.770
and you can see that the first principle component
drops dramatically in February of 2003, and

00:40:31.770 --> 00:40:40.390
then it drops again in April, and then it
kind of stays there. So maybe they were buying

00:40:40.390 --> 00:40:44.900
boxes of whatever-- we don't know what it
was, but you can definitely see that there's

00:40:44.900 --> 00:40:50.960
a big change within the group, which is explained
by month.

00:40:50.960 --> 00:40:56.190
So when I see a picture like this, I say well,
I want to do this experiment again. I want

00:40:56.190 --> 00:41:02.710
to balance everything and see if I get a different
answer. All right, so now let's explain this

00:41:02.710 --> 00:41:09.580
with inference and statistical modeling, this
batch effect, which is ubiquitous in high

00:41:09.580 --> 00:41:17.370
throughput technology measurements and beyond.
One thing I should mention as an aside, we

00:41:17.370 --> 00:41:23.960
can see the batch effect very clearly in high
throughput technology. In low throughput technologies,

00:41:23.960 --> 00:41:29.670
it's not always obvious, but it's harder to
see if it is there.

00:41:29.670 --> 00:41:34.660
So in a way, high throughput technologies
maybe being negatively affected because of

00:41:34.660 --> 00:41:40.670
the fact they reveal something. Because they're
more informative, you actually can see the

00:41:40.670 --> 00:41:44.580
problems. When it's not as informative, it's
low throughput, you might be there, but you

00:41:44.580 --> 00:41:49.500
don't see it. So don't think this is just
a problem with high throughput data, it's

00:41:49.500 --> 00:41:52.560
just that we can see it with high throughput
data. I'm going to give you an example of

00:41:52.560 --> 00:41:55.470
a low throughput
experiment that had a huge batch effect in

00:41:55.470 --> 00:41:59.070
a second.
All right, so here's a general-- just write

00:41:59.070 --> 00:42:06.140
it down with the inference model, with a statistical
modeling approach of why this is a problem,

00:42:06.140 --> 00:42:11.390
and I'm going to write it in more general
terms. So I'm interested in the difference

00:42:11.390 --> 00:42:15.100
between two groups. In this case, it was too
ethnic groups, but it could be something else.

00:42:15.100 --> 00:42:22.710
We earlier did two strains of mice, and before
that we did two sexes or genders.

00:42:22.710 --> 00:42:26.570
So you have two groups, group one and group
two, and we're going to take several measurements

00:42:26.570 --> 00:42:30.570
for each one. We're going to take several
individuals from each one. So that's what

00:42:30.570 --> 00:42:36.110
the i is indexing. So we have several measurements
for one, several measurements for two, and

00:42:36.110 --> 00:42:42.300
what I am assuming is that-- or the way I'm
modeling this is that there there's this mean.

00:42:42.300 --> 00:42:47.140
There's a mean value for that group, alpha,
and then there's differences between individuals,

00:42:47.140 --> 00:42:51.000
which are described with this term here. This
is sometimes called measurement error, but

00:42:51.000 --> 00:42:55.841
in the case of heights or gene expression,
it's other things. It's not just measurement

00:42:55.841 --> 00:42:59.610
error in the case of heights. Some people
are tall so they have a big epsilon, some

00:42:59.610 --> 00:43:07.310
people are short so they have a negative epsilon.
OK, then the second group has the same mean,

00:43:07.310 --> 00:43:12.650
and perhaps, that's what we're going to test,
it has a difference that is parametrized with

00:43:12.650 --> 00:43:17.400
the beta, and then they also have measurement
error. So what I'm looking for, I'm looking

00:43:17.400 --> 00:43:24.810
for genes or-- in the previous case, I was
looking for genes that had betas different

00:43:24.810 --> 00:43:29.440
from zero, and reporting those. All right,
so how do I find beta? We already went over

00:43:29.440 --> 00:43:33.640
this, but here we go again.
The difference between the two means of the

00:43:33.640 --> 00:43:40.250
two groups is going to be approximately beta.
The alphas cancel out. The epsilons average

00:43:40.250 --> 00:43:45.000
out to zero if we take enough measurements.
So this is approximately beta, than what we

00:43:45.000 --> 00:43:48.680
did with the t-test is to go further and say,
well, it's beta, but we also know that it

00:43:48.680 --> 00:43:54.640
has a standard error, which has to do with
the population variance and the size of the

00:43:54.640 --> 00:43:58.690
sample. So that's the standard statistical
approach to doing this, and that's what that

00:43:58.690 --> 00:44:03.450
was done for the previous example we talked
about when they were looking for genes that

00:44:03.450 --> 00:44:09.830
were different in the two groups.
Now, when you have a batch effect, that model

00:44:09.830 --> 00:44:18.220
is no longer correct. Instead-- and here I'm
showing an example where the batch effect

00:44:18.220 --> 00:44:23.810
was confounded, meaning that group one was
done in one batch, group two was in another

00:44:23.810 --> 00:44:31.830
batch. So this W that I'm showing you here,
is the potential difference introduced by

00:44:31.830 --> 00:44:38.890
being in a different batch. Note that there's
no i. So each batch has its own mean value.

00:44:38.890 --> 00:44:47.210
So if I have this, if this is my correct model
then I have a problem because this is no longer

00:44:47.210 --> 00:44:55.480
the case. This should be alpha, sorry, this
guy here. The alphas no longer cancel out.

00:44:55.480 --> 00:45:02.950
I'm sorry, the alphas do cancel out, the epsilons
still average out to zero, but these W's,

00:45:02.950 --> 00:45:09.530
they stay. So when I look at the difference
in the average, what I'm seeing

00:45:09.530 --> 00:45:17.440
is-- the difference I'm interested in plus
the difference in the average values introduced

00:45:17.440 --> 00:45:20.140
by the batches.
So if I look at these differences and I see

00:45:20.140 --> 00:45:26.921
a five, I don't know if it's two plus three,
or zero plus five, or five plus zero. I have

00:45:26.921 --> 00:45:33.900
no way of distinguishing that. I have no information.
I have no way of separating that when it's

00:45:33.900 --> 00:45:43.070
completely confounded. That's the problem
with confounding batches with outcomes, and

00:45:43.070 --> 00:45:46.810
unfortunately that happens a lot in these
experiments where they get all the controls,

00:45:46.810 --> 00:45:49.700
and they run them first, and they then get
all the treatments and run a second. I see

00:45:49.700 --> 00:45:55.620
it all the time, this is the type of confounding.
Another way to think about this, for those

00:45:55.620 --> 00:45:59.500
that are more advanced statistically, is to
think that these Ws are random variables,

00:45:59.500 --> 00:46:08.920
so it's like a random effect that each batch
introduces. It's the same across batches,

00:46:08.920 --> 00:46:15.510
so the way that this is then turns into a
description of the problem is that the variance

00:46:15.510 --> 00:46:20.930
that we estimated for our estimate is bigger
than the variance due to W, than what we actually

00:46:20.930 --> 00:46:25.280
use for a t-test, which is just this component.
So when we construct a t-test with this variance,

00:46:25.280 --> 00:46:31.510
were underestimating the variance, and therefore
our t-test are bigger than they should. That's

00:46:31.510 --> 00:46:37.870
another way to thinking about this.
OK, so here's an example. Earlier I said this

00:46:37.870 --> 00:46:44.650
is not specific to high throughput. Here's
an example from physics experiments from the

00:46:44.650 --> 00:46:52.740
last century. This is data that you can get
from a paper. They're published in Technometrics

00:46:52.740 --> 00:46:58.210
by Yuden in 1972. He actually has a table
of these values, and I turned them into a

00:46:58.210 --> 00:47:05.530
figure. And they are the reported estimates
of the astronomical unit along with a confidence

00:47:05.530 --> 00:47:09.970
interval.
So this is what today we think is the astronomical

00:47:09.970 --> 00:47:19.740
unit, is shown here with this dash line, and
here is the first experiment-- at least in

00:47:19.740 --> 00:47:24.800
this paper was reported, and then here's the
second one, and we can see that the speed

00:47:24.800 --> 00:47:29.640
of light-- there's something wrong with either
the estimates or the confidence intervals,

00:47:29.640 --> 00:47:35.770
or the speed of light changed in 1900. And
you can see it happen again, right? Because

00:47:35.770 --> 00:47:40.580
this is definitely statistically significant.
Here is it again, it changes again in 1920,

00:47:40.580 --> 00:47:47.560
the speed of light changed.
So that is a problem, and if we just looked

00:47:47.560 --> 00:47:51.300
at this guy with no other information, we
wouldn't know what's going on. But once we

00:47:51.300 --> 00:47:55.720
make the whole plot, now he turned it into
a high throughput experiment with many labs

00:47:55.720 --> 00:48:01.790
measuring it, we see the problem, and it's
that there's a lab effect. It's a batch effect.

00:48:01.790 --> 00:48:08.430
Depending on what lab did it, we get a deviation
from what today is considered to be the right

00:48:08.430 --> 00:48:13.930
answer.
The lab that we're measuring these, within

00:48:13.930 --> 00:48:18.000
their lab they can't see that they have this
batch effect, so the confidence intervals

00:48:18.000 --> 00:48:23.090
that they report are much smaller than what
they should be. So here's another way to think

00:48:23.090 --> 00:48:31.780
about it. This variance that we see here is
estimated as a measurement error variance.

00:48:31.780 --> 00:48:36.632
So we get it from within the experiment. That
would be the variance of the measurement error,

00:48:36.632 --> 00:48:41.960
while the batch effect variance, w, is much
bigger. That's why the points are varying

00:48:41.960 --> 00:48:48.390
all over the place.
All right. There are some solutions that have

00:48:48.390 --> 00:48:58.120
been developed for this problem. They're on
something called factor analysis. It's actually

00:48:58.120 --> 00:49:06.410
a very old technique from the early 1900s,
and the reason that this technique is widely

00:49:06.410 --> 00:49:12.330
used is because when we look at the data--
this is a plot of the correlations between

00:49:12.330 --> 00:49:21.440
the 200 individuals from the previous example,
and you can see that there's blocks of groups

00:49:21.440 --> 00:49:27.920
that are more correlated to each other.
This must be the first two years, and this

00:49:27.920 --> 00:49:32.020
is like 2005 because you can see these blocks
of time, but then there's sub blocks. There's

00:49:32.020 --> 00:49:39.830
all this structure. We see tight red dots,
not as tight, but somewhat pinkish big squares.

00:49:39.830 --> 00:49:47.200
So there's a structure in the data that is
induced by some kind of weird grouping that

00:49:47.200 --> 00:49:51.580
has to do with date, but it's not clear that
it's just date. So when you have a problem

00:49:51.580 --> 00:49:56.950
like that, factoring analysis, it tries to
estimate this structure and then take it into

00:49:56.950 --> 00:50:00.970
account when you're doing the analysis. So
I'm going to try to explain that in five minutes

00:50:00.970 --> 00:50:04.050
so that we can finish with five minutes for
questions.

00:50:04.050 --> 00:50:10.930
And to describe this approach and give you
an idea of how complex this is, I'm going

00:50:10.930 --> 00:50:18.600
to use a subset of the earlier data set of
200 people. I'm going to take 24 of them.

00:50:18.600 --> 00:50:25.480
I'm going to take 12 men, and 12 females,
and 12 males. So you can see the experimental

00:50:25.480 --> 00:50:31.130
design here. I'm going to confound date, but
not completely. So these techniques work when

00:50:31.130 --> 00:50:36.850
they-- if something is completely confounded,
we currently don't have any solutions, but

00:50:36.850 --> 00:50:42.480
if it's not, you can use statistical models
to help.

00:50:42.480 --> 00:50:46.880
You can see the confounding here. More males
were measured in June than females, and more

00:50:46.880 --> 00:50:51.320
females were measured in October than males.
All right, so this plot-- this is not a cartoon.

00:50:51.320 --> 00:50:58.720
This is, again, actual data from the experimental
data. You can see genes. I have selected some

00:50:58.720 --> 00:51:06.040
genes. And I'm not showing you all 8,000,
and you can see the y-axis up here-- sorry,

00:51:06.040 --> 00:51:12.140
the Y chromosome up here. These are genes
on the Y chromosome. Males are high, females

00:51:12.140 --> 00:51:17.810
and low because females don't have a Y chromosome.
All right, so there are the 12 man and the

00:51:17.810 --> 00:51:22.930
12 women, and 12 males, 12 females, and these
are genes on the Y chromosome.

00:51:22.930 --> 00:51:27.880
Then we see other genes and there's no difference.
You can see some variability, and then down

00:51:27.880 --> 00:51:33.810
here you can see the dates. These are genes
that are affected by the batch effect. So

00:51:33.810 --> 00:51:38.650
let's look at that. So can you tell where
June is? You can see it here. See this square

00:51:38.650 --> 00:51:43.951
here? There's nine people here, nine men.
These nine men were done in June, they're

00:51:43.951 --> 00:51:52.180
right here, and then these three men, they're
right here. They're blue. So you see October

00:51:52.180 --> 00:52:02.990
and June. This is June and October. For the
females, there's three in June and 9 in October.

00:52:02.990 --> 00:52:07.300
So you can see batch effect.
So what happens if I do a t-test now, just

00:52:07.300 --> 00:52:11.980
ignoring this? If I compare this side to that
side, these are going to be super significant,

00:52:11.980 --> 00:52:18.540
but so are these because there's more red
here than over here. This is another way of

00:52:18.540 --> 00:52:24.470
writing out the t-test model. If I assume
this, this area is going to cause problems

00:52:24.470 --> 00:52:31.530
for me. If I make a histogram of pvalues I
get a lot of significant genes. When I look

00:52:31.530 --> 00:52:34.550
at the autosome genes, I'm really not expecting
many, or any at

00:52:34.550 --> 00:52:42.770
all. It's driven probably by the fact that
we have a difference between June and October.

00:52:42.770 --> 00:52:49.110
So, you can see the sample correlations again
here. Here's the original ordering with females

00:52:49.110 --> 00:52:55.540
and males, ordered right like that. The correlation
is not explained by it, it's explained by

00:52:55.540 --> 00:53:03.070
date. So if I order them by date, you can
see June and October, that there's two big

00:53:03.070 --> 00:53:07.360
red boxes. There's more correlation in the
ones within October, more correlation than

00:53:07.360 --> 00:53:13.040
the ones in June, but we can't just model
it as June in October because there's sub

00:53:13.040 --> 00:53:18.620
groups. So there's another subgroup here that
apparently ended in October 23, or right before,

00:53:18.620 --> 00:53:24.450
or in the morning of June 23, and then it
stops, and then there's another little box

00:53:24.450 --> 00:53:28.950
here. We can see some tiny-
Basically what I'm saying is it's not as simple

00:53:28.950 --> 00:53:33.682
as just dates. There's some other thing going
on. What factor analysis does is it tries

00:53:33.682 --> 00:53:41.090
to estimate this structure. So what we do
is we add another component, it's a linear

00:53:41.090 --> 00:53:48.040
component, for those that know what I mean
by that, and it basically tries to model this

00:53:48.040 --> 00:53:57.720
structure with a linear model where you're
actually estimating the matrix that describes

00:53:57.720 --> 00:54:06.740
the variability. This is a little more complicated.
PCA works for estimating these components,

00:54:06.740 --> 00:54:10.360
and that's what basically factor analysis
does.

00:54:10.360 --> 00:54:15.100
The reason there's publications in 2007 as
opposed to just saying let's just use factor

00:54:15.100 --> 00:54:22.910
analysis because we have to now distinguish
between being a model-- we have to model the

00:54:22.910 --> 00:54:26.950
part that has to do with the biology that
we care about. So we want to estimate the

00:54:26.950 --> 00:54:33.460
difference between males and females.
We don't want that to just be thrown into

00:54:33.460 --> 00:54:39.560
the factor part, and then we model this part
down here with a factor model, and then estimating

00:54:39.560 --> 00:54:44.690
and fitting this model is complicated, and
that's why there's publications because there's

00:54:44.690 --> 00:54:50.930
different approaches for doing this and different
techniques. There's several papers. But here's

00:54:50.930 --> 00:54:56.550
the result of applying one of them. This is
now flattened out. That's good, it means it

00:54:56.550 --> 00:55:00.220
removed most of the genes that we think were
due to the batch effect. That's what happens

00:55:00.220 --> 00:55:06.260
when you apply this model to this data. We
reduce the number of false positives by 100.

00:55:06.260 --> 00:55:13.480
So here's what it's doing. This factor analysis
approach is splitting the data up into the

00:55:13.480 --> 00:55:17.060
differences due to sex. You can see them up
here. I think these are two false positives,

00:55:17.060 --> 00:55:22.130
or maybe not. But you have the two differences
in sex, then you have the batch effect here,

00:55:22.130 --> 00:55:25.940
and now you have the measurement error over
here. So it breaks down the data pretty well

00:55:25.940 --> 00:55:32.060
into the different parts.
All right, so-- is that repeated? It is repeated,

00:55:32.060 --> 00:55:38.950
sorry. So this is all we have. We wrote a
review on these methods. It was published

00:55:38.950 --> 00:55:48.460
about five years ago, maybe more, and you
can go read it. It's described in relatively

00:55:48.460 --> 00:55:54.290
simple terms so that you can be aware of what
the problem is, and some of the solutions

00:55:54.290 --> 00:55:58.170
that exist. But this is an area of current
research. In fact, I'm doing a lot of research

00:55:58.170 --> 00:56:02.830
today related to solving that problem in different
technologies. Three examples listed here.

00:56:02.830 --> 00:56:08.640
If you want to see more, you can follow--
I'll announce whatever we put out on Twitter,

00:56:08.640 --> 00:56:13.200
and also at some point the links go up on
our web page.

00:56:13.200 --> 00:56:17.340
All right, so I want to stop there and thank
some of the people that helped me with the

00:56:17.340 --> 00:56:22.493
research I described today, and of course
the NIH for funding a lot of this work. OK,

00:56:22.493 --> 00:56:29.390
that's the end.
Great. Thank you Dr. Irizarry for this wonderful

00:56:29.390 --> 00:56:33.880
start to section four for our data science
seminars. I think we have time for one quick

00:56:33.880 --> 00:56:39.010
question. It's a little bit of a provocative
question, but the question is related to sort

00:56:39.010 --> 00:56:46.570
of the natural tension that exists between
exploratory data analysis and knowledge discovery,

00:56:46.570 --> 00:56:53.220
and whether there's sort of-- what is the
sort of natural balance between discovering

00:56:53.220 --> 00:57:01.600
knowledge simply through data science and
needing additional confirmatory analyses or

00:57:01.600 --> 00:57:04.510
experiments? Could you comment a little bit
on that maybe?

00:57:04.510 --> 00:57:16.350
Me? Oh, sure. The way I work, I always work
with collaborators that understand the context

00:57:16.350 --> 00:57:24.850
and the substance-- I shouldn't say substance,
but the subject matter that we're studying,

00:57:24.850 --> 00:57:31.910
and that helps creates-- greatly helps make
sure that what we're finding makes some sense,

00:57:31.910 --> 00:57:40.800
that it has some explanations that are grounded
in existing knowledge. And of course, we definitely

00:57:40.800 --> 00:57:48.740
encourage our collaborators to do confirmation
analyses when possible. I'm a big believer

00:57:48.740 --> 00:58:01.320
on confirmation and on replication.
I like the way that science becomes the--

00:58:01.320 --> 00:58:08.820
what's the word that's used? The scientific
consensus, the way that we come to in the

00:58:08.820 --> 00:58:13.560
United States today, it's not just papers
published and we believe it. It's not two

00:58:13.560 --> 00:58:17.710
papers are published and we believe it. It
usually takes much more than that. It sometimes

00:58:17.710 --> 00:58:23.730
takes a group of experts convened by the National
Academy to sit down and read everything that's

00:58:23.730 --> 00:58:27.250
been published, think hard, and put out some
statement.

00:58:27.250 --> 00:58:33.250
So in general, I think that the person who
asked that question is worried that we just

00:58:33.250 --> 00:58:40.450
we explore the data and find some serendipitous
result and publish it. I don't think that's

00:58:40.450 --> 00:58:45.150
how we should do it. We should definitely
collaborate with experts, and we should also

00:58:45.150 --> 00:58:50.050
confirm and look to future papers to see if
our results are confirmed or not.

00:58:50.050 --> 00:58:59.180
But with that said, I'm a big believer that
data exploration can lead to discovery, and

00:58:59.180 --> 00:59:05.000
also on the other side, it can also lead to
the discovery of problems, of issues with

00:59:05.000 --> 00:59:09.710
something that's been published because the
data had problems that weren't caught by the

00:59:09.710 --> 00:59:12.510
standard workflow. So I hope that answers
the question.

00:59:12.510 --> 00:59:19.740
No, I think that's a great answer and certainly
insightful. I think looking at the body of

00:59:19.740 --> 00:59:25.531
work, rather than just a single sort of discovery
is really how science operates today, and

00:59:25.531 --> 00:59:30.200
I think that's an important point that you've
made. So thank you again, so much for your

00:59:30.200 --> 00:59:34.340
time, and for giving this talk. We're at 10:00,
so we'll conclude here.

00:59:34.340 --> 00:59:37.210
Great, thank you. Bye bye.
Bye.

